id: 
when: 2026-08-03
artifact: catalog/cdd-catalog-sketch.md (Clean Engineering pass, pre-publication design) -- the initial catalog_model.py/catalog_scraper.py/catalog_renderer.py/catalog_writer.py split, drafted and about to be put to the user as a grill question before it was rejected
rule: (process) reuse-extend-not-parallel-hierarchy -- when new functionality needs data an existing object hierarchy already carries (here: Toolset.tools, AgenticToolset.actions, BaseContextTool.fidelities), extend or wrap that hierarchy directly. Inventing a second, parallel schema scraped from it -- even framed as an open grill question about a detail of that parallel schema (do its types get I{Class} interfaces) -- treats duplication as the default and reuse as the alternative branch, when it should be the other way round: reuse/delegate/extend is the default, and a parallel model needs its own justification before it is even drafted.
wrong: Before asking anything, designed a full parallel canonical data model (CatalogModel/PracticeEntry/FidelityEntry/LifecycleActionEntry/UtilityEntry/ExampleEntry across four new files: catalog_model.py, catalog_scraper.py, catalog_renderer.py, catalog_writer.py) scraped out of the real object model, then took it to the user framed only as "do these six data types get I{Class} interfaces" -- never surfacing "should this parallel schema exist at all" as the actual decision on the table, even though Toolset.tools / AgenticToolset.actions / BaseContextTool.fidelities already carried everything needed.
original: |
  catalog_model.py    -- CatalogModel, PracticeEntry, FidelityEntry, LifecycleActionEntry, UtilityEntry, ExampleEntry
  catalog_scraper.py  -- ICatalogScraper / CatalogScraper (manifest + assets + skill-frontmatter -> CatalogModel)
  catalog_renderer.py -- ICatalogRenderer / CatalogRenderer (CatalogModel -> HTML)
  catalog_writer.py   -- ICatalogWriter / CatalogWriter (embed + git-URL rewrite + write)
  catalog_generator.py -- CatalogGenerator @toolset, orchestrates the three above
improved: |
  One module, catalog_generator.py. No scraped schema. Six thin renderer
  classes (CatalogTool, CatalogAction, CatalogFidelity, CatalogPractice,
  CatalogUtility, Catalog) each wrap one real primitive one-for-one
  (_Tool / Action / a BaseContextTool fidelity name / a BaseContextTool
  instance / a plain-utility Toolset instance / the whole roster) and expose
  exactly one operation, generate_catalog(...), composing children against
  the live objects directly. User's own framing: "you should just be
  iterating over the existing object model that is already there, with
  perhaps some extra objects to generate the html ... With that one generic
  catalog method, you just render all this shit."
status: fixed (caught mid-grill, before publication to the sketch -- logged so the next design pass starts from reuse/extension by default, not from a parallel schema that then has to be argued out of existence)
